{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-\u00e6on/494207-my-aeon-marathon","id":494207,"title":"My Aeon Marathon","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Trinity Continuum","Trinity Continuum: \u00c6on"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-04-27T18:49:17-07:00","id":494207,"title":"My Aeon Marathon","content":"As promised on the Max Mercer thread, I've dug up my notes from my Aeon Marathon campaign. For those who are not aware, the Aeon Marathon was my name for the idea of running a long campaign with the same group taking related characters from Adventure!, to Aberrant, and finally, to Trinity. This was a long game, taking the better part of 5 years and ending almost that long ago. Unfortunately, my notes and my character sheets have not survived well in the 3 moves since, so this will be more of a summary than play-by-play.<br /> <br /> The Adventure! game was mostly fairly standard. The crew fought evil pulp villains (including a masked mastermind with a Zeppelin and a small army of goons, as well as a daredevil mob boss), filled in the map (including rescuing an indigenous population on an island from evil), and set the foundations for the Aeon Society as a whole. They worked closely with the founders (even growing to like Dr. Primoris), and eventually took part (there were some very pulpy time skips) in the battle against Primoris in the end. The characters included a wealthy Indian psychomorph, a daredevil private detective loosely based on the Question, and an heiress adventurer stalwart.<br /> <br /> We flashed forward to just after N-Day. The characters had passed on, but their descendants had erupted. The wealthy Indian psychomorph was replaced by a radiant godling who straddled the line between Utopian and Teragen, a Batman-esque frost-powered defender, and an adrenaline junkie conjuring animal companions from pure quantum. This began fairly standard, superheroes working for T2M to make the world better, at the guidance of their Utopia handler. Over time, however, they found themselves ordered to investigate leads that led more and more to the discovery of the problems in the setting, including the Directive and, eventually, Project Proteus. Their handler was Mercer in disguise, trying to guide the descendants of his friends and allies to Proteus so that they could, as being part of the timeline and not outside of it, fix what he could not. I was fully prepared to run a more limited Aberrant War, one with novas actively fighting on both sides with more limited devastation, but they let the word about Proteus slip (in fairness, I ambushed them with an undercover reporter, just to see what would happen). Proteus had been dismantled by this point, and some Aeon-loyal novas with low Taint tried to force back the destructive aberrants, but it was insufficient to do more than protect some landmarks and leave behind some folk legends of &quot;the aberrants that saved us from their evil brethren&quot; in some areas.<br /> <br /> Final flash forward led to the Trinity era, shortly after the return of the Upeo. The novas could not have children, obviously, so we were dealing with nieces and nephews, for the most part. Clearly, they had suffered a bit in their upbringing as a result of their associations. One was a biokinetic freedom fighter, another a loyal Orgotek employee whose father's hard tech business had been purchased early on, and the last was a vitakinetic with the mindset of a dedicated Doctor Without Borders. This time, Max was again their handler, arranged again because of his memories of their Aeon ancestors, but he was honest with them from the beginning about who he was and how he knew them. The various missions were designed to explore the setting (including slight variations on most of the premade adventures, foiling an FSA plot to engineer &quot;terrorist attacks&quot; near the Orgotek New York arcology to stagger the psions there enough to finally bring them to heel, and a brief, paranoid visit to Nippon), but mostly, Max was curious about the Doyen and finding their motivations. He is a humanist and humanitarian, and the seemingly amoral, selfish, and manipulative Doyen fell more and more into the character's crosshairs as time went on. Eden made an appearance during the Chromatic conflict, although I used a council of nameless novas to replace Apollo Milliken. The game unfortunately petered out before it could reach a real conclusion, but the crew had found, in the final session, a lone, half-mad aberrant in a faded Team Tomorrow uniform who claimed to know the whereabouts of their famous ancestors, who could perhaps explain why their families were so blessed/cursed.<br /> <br /> It was an insane amount of fun, and the players all made considerable impacts to the flow of the timeline. Mostly, they served as the conscience for Aeon, trusted allies of Max Mercer throughout the centuries for keeping rogue elements in the society on the righteous path, and trying to find the truth of the real enemies of mankind from each setting. I only wish I still had all my notes and had written where the story was going, but it was quite the ride while it lasted.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4732-drazosh","name":"Drazosh","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"50"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-27T20:43:40-07:00","id":494305,"title":"","content":"This sounds like it would have been a blast, i'm envious of both yourself and Lustral_Star (another poster who has done all three settings with the same characters in his case, if i understood him) for having run these games. A few things require an immediate explanation as far as i can see:<br /> How did you handle the conflict between Mercer and Donighal after the 1943 battle with Primoris, was there a climax?<br /> How did Mercer age in between the eras, how old (by his perspective) was he by the end of the campaign?<br /> Did you use any pulp Adventure setting elements in 1998+?<br /> Who was Michael Mercer? Did the play ever intersect him or his daughter?<br /> You said in the Max Mercer thread that Mercer could never really be rid of Proteus by himself, but you have it dismantled here - is this because of the PCs?<br /> How did you explain the difference between 2008 Proteus and 2120 Proteus to the players and did they ever really trust the new Proteus?<br /> Where do you think you might have taken the 'famous ancestor' plot, and how would you have run the Coalition invasion?<br /> Did you reach the point of Process 418/Venezuela effect?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4458,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4458-nihilist","user":"Nihilist","date":".  moar questions 04-28-2015, 12:38 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4458-nihilist","name":"Nihilist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4458_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-27T21:39:10-07:00","id":494356,"title":"","content":"I really enjoyed the campaign. Max gave me the opportunity to play with the timeline in ways I never would have expected before. I'll answer in order.<br /> <br /> 1. Truthfully, that event marked the end of the Adventure! segment of the campaign. Two of the three characters suffered fairly serious injuries during the battle (actually one health level apiece from dead), and it seemed like a good place for them to bow out gracefully from active work.<br /> 2. Like I said, I played with Mercer's timeline quite a bit, and it would vary from appearance to appearance. He had a habit of setting up the mission that would start a story off, and disappear. There were times where they needed some intervention from their local Aeon rep, and not only would he not be around, but no one would have even heard of the pseudonym he gave them. Other times, he'd leave them cryptic messages in a centuries-old safety deposit box (on Proteus: &quot;I'm afraid I might never kill the beast of many shapes.&quot;) As important as he was as my tool to steer the campaign when it needed direction, he spent a lot of time absent, and even occasionally turning up as a different age and giving seemingly contradictory information, so making sense of it all was part of the fun.<br /> 3. There were actually moments where descendants and spiritual successors of Adventure elements played recurring roles (The great grandson of the thoroughly deposed daredevil mobster, for instance, was their informant in the New York arcology fiasco). Overall, though, I tried to keep the genres distinct to keep things straight in my own head as well as the players'.<br /> 3.5. (Re: The Mercer clan) Michael was mostly during a time skip, although I always envisioned him being much like his father. Perhaps a little less brave, a little less dogged, but still devoted to the cause. His daughter came up a bit in 1998+, of course, but more as a distant and ambiguous pseudo-antagonist who meant well, but let those good intentions pave the road to Hell.<br /> 4. My general rule of thumb when running a game is this: NPCs might struggle and fail to fix problems in the setting because of fate/the capacity for history to protect itself at fixed points/what have you. PCs are the stars BECAUSE they have the skill and determination to shape the future. In this case, Max needed heroes from that time to get to the bottom of what key individuals in Aeon were hiding from him (With a reaction like the one I described in the Max thread), and put a stop to it if need be. As a time hopping adventurer, his ability to directly affect events out of his own time was limited.<br /> 5. That was actually a derailing moment in the story that I allowed. They did not trust the Proteus revival, even though its intentions and methods were very different, and they took the time to make sure. While much of the Proteus history was edited out, their families would have passed on some information, and they understandably (if somewhat OOCly) handled its reincarnation with suspicion.<br /> 6. It could have gone a number of different ways. I try to steer stories in a direction, gauge reactions, and alter from there. Ultimately, I was going for a chicken/egg approach, where THEIR descendants in the distant future traveled back, met Max Mercer during the tail end of his life and, to help him in his goals, seeded their family DNA to react to the Hammersmith incident and related quantum and subquantum events. Thus, the families he came to rely upon so much were specifically put there for him to rely upon, by allies he would only meet at the end of his life. Alternately, because I have a habit of coming up with multiple ideas, jotting them down, and only making my final decision at the table, there were in fact only 3 characters (as opposed to the 9 there had been, by this point), who had scattered themselves across time, a sort of anthropomorphic Aeon Trinity working towards the greater good. Perhaps a bit overblown, I'll freely admit that, but these players and their characters have been there for 200+ years of the Aeon Society. They've helped fill in the map of the Earth, changed it for the better, and then moved on to map the stars. They'd fought gangsters, demigods, and everything in between. They should have been, needed to be, important.<br /> 6a. As for the Coalition... I wanted to use them, as they fit the &quot;impending alien invasion&quot; trope so very well... But their description is inherently programmed to make the majority of gaming groups uncomfortable, so I was trying to find a way to include them without the joys of explaining that, yes, the core book describes them as space rape monsters, but that's frankly just a cheap shot at the shock value angle and I can do better than that. Ultimately, I chose to save them for later, which never properly happened.<br /> 7. I didn't, sadly, which could have been interesting in a number of ways. In light of what the characters now knew about their family lines, what could happen with another incident? They presumably have other relatives; 200+ years after the start of it all, they probably have some they've never even met. Do they run home and check on their relatives? If a family member becomes an aberrant, how do they respond? Do they even know if the fresh aberrant they're hunting is family? It varies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4732,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4732-drazosh","user":"Drazosh","date":".  Answer most questions, and alt 6 04-28-2015, 01:16 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4732-drazosh","name":"Drazosh","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"50"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-28T03:13:16-07:00","id":494562,"title":"","content":"I'll pepper you with questions if you let me, answer the ones that interest you and move on if i'm getting tiresome.<br /> -smile-<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Drazosh</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-\u00e6on/494207-my-aeon-marathon?p=494356#post494356\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I really enjoyed the campaign. Max gave me the opportunity to play with the timeline in ways I never would have expected before. I'll answer in order.<br /> <br /> 1. Truthfully, that event marked the end of the Adventure! segment of the campaign. Two of the three characters suffered fairly serious injuries during the battle (actually one health level apiece from dead), and it seemed like a good place for them to bow out gracefully from active work.</div> </div> </div> </div>So you left the conflict between Donighal and Mercer behind after the '43 battle? They didn't interact again in the Aberrant years? The Battle with Primoris certainly makes a good bookend for the golden years - i see you jumped forward at that point, but can you give an idea of some of the changes that took place in the society during that down time between eras? At least in how you ran it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 2. Like I said, I played with Mercer's timeline quite a bit, and it would vary from appearance to appearance. He had a habit of setting up the mission that would start a story off, and disappear. There were times where they needed some intervention from their local Aeon rep, and not only would he not be around, but no one would have even heard of the pseudonym he gave them. Other times, he'd leave them cryptic messages in a centuries-old safety deposit box (on Proteus: &quot;I'm afraid I might never kill the beast of many shapes.&quot;) As important as he was as my tool to steer the campaign when it needed direction, he spent a lot of time absent, and even occasionally turning up as a different age and giving seemingly contradictory information, so making sense of it all was part of the fun. </div> </div> </div>Did you have an overall plan for Mercer's lifetime? What was the oldest they ever encountered Max at, what was the youngest? If you have an overall picture of Maxwells travels, let us know in the Mercer thread - could be fun to consider. When were the character first introduced to the time-travel aspect of the character? Did you run him much the same in his native time?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3. There were actually moments where descendants and spiritual successors of Adventure elements played recurring roles (The great grandson of the thoroughly deposed daredevil mobster, for instance, was their informant in the New York arcology fiasco). Overall, though, I tried to keep the genres distinct to keep things straight in my own head as well as the players'. </div> </div> </div>Nice. I feel that Aberrant lacks the ties to Adventure! that Trinity had to Aberrant, so i had to check. I can see the genre shift in the characters you listed, so you did a decent job there based on the evidence provided. How about Daredevil/Paramorphs in general, were they a useful tool in any era outside of Adventure!?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3.5. (Re: The Mercer clan) Michael was mostly during a time skip, although I always envisioned him being much like his father. Perhaps a little less brave, a little less dogged, but still devoted to the cause. His daughter came up a bit in 1998+, of course, but more as a distant and ambiguous pseudo-antagonist who meant well, but let those good intentions pave the road to Hell. </div> </div> </div>I'm unfamiliar with Margaret, because i never got hands on the d20 books. But Michael Mercer is one of the bigger loose threads left in the Adventure! meta-plot, so he is an interesting character.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 4. My general rule of thumb when running a game is this: NPCs might struggle and fail to fix problems in the setting because of fate/the capacity for history to protect itself at fixed points/what have you. PCs are the stars BECAUSE they have the skill and determination to shape the future. In this case, Max needed heroes from that time to get to the bottom of what key individuals in Aeon were hiding from him (With a reaction like the one I described in the Max thread), and put a stop to it if need be. As a time hopping adventurer, his ability to directly affect events out of his own time was limited. </div> </div> </div>This comes perilously close to how i would have run Maxwells Chronal Awareness, it also links in with why he started the society as soon as he gets back - so he can make changes by proxy - because he himself is mostly just a witness outside of his living years. I won't get too deep into it here, but that sounds very familiar and playable to me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 5. That was actually a derailing moment in the story that I allowed. They did not trust the Proteus revival, even though its intentions and methods were very different, and they took the time to make sure. While much of the Proteus history was edited out, their families would have passed on some information, and they understandably (if somewhat OOCly) handled its reincarnation with suspicion. </div> </div> </div>I ran tainted Novas out of 2120 and they were able to 'quantum' their way around Project Rewrite to get at some history - but that was a cheap workaround for a couple of players acting on OOC knowledge. Even when they play Psions, i can't wipe the stains of 2120 Proteus and the mistrust can get outright disruptive. Glad to hear it wasn't just me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 6. It could have gone a number of different ways. I try to steer stories in a direction, gauge reactions, and alter from there. Ultimately, I was going for a chicken/egg approach, where THEIR descendants in the distant future traveled back, met Max Mercer during the tail end of his life and, to help him in his goals, seeded their family DNA to react to the Hammersmith incident and related quantum and subquantum events. Thus, the families he came to rely upon so much were specifically put there for him to rely upon, by allies he would only meet at the end of his life. Alternately, because I have a habit of coming up with multiple ideas, jotting them down, and only making my final decision at the table, there were in fact only 3 characters (as opposed to the 9 there had been, by this point), who had scattered themselves across time, a sort of anthropomorphic Aeon Trinity working towards the greater good. Perhaps a bit overblown, I'll freely admit that, but these players and their characters have been there for 200+ years of the Aeon Society. They've helped fill in the map of the Earth, changed it for the better, and then moved on to map the stars. They'd fought gangsters, demigods, and everything in between. They should have been, needed to be, important. </div> </div> </div>A cyclical conclusion, nice. I don't mind a good one of those.<br /> For all the focus placed on the signature characters of the setting, the ultimate protagonists are the PCs, if your big conclusion focussed anywhere else it would have been folly. I also tend to work from loose flowchart-style adventures and like to leave my options open until the final moment of decision - ST/GM/DM/whatever-ing can be entirely psychological with such a captive and shallow audience. Shallow in terms of numbers not mentally.<br /> I like it, a bit cosmic, but this is toward the conclusion to a two-century long story - you <i>need</i> to go big.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 6a. As for the Coalition... I wanted to use them, as they fit the &quot;impending alien invasion&quot; trope so very well... But their description is inherently programmed to make the majority of gaming groups uncomfortable, so I was trying to find a way to include them without the joys of explaining that, yes, the core book describes them as space rape monsters, but that's frankly just a cheap shot at the shock value angle and I can do better than that. Ultimately, I chose to save them for later, which never properly happened. </div> </div> </div>I've seen folks submit their discomfort at the Coalition first encounter - it's entirely natural. The word 'rape' however is a slight misnomer as far as i saw it - it might be more accurate to call it a living biological vivisection for genetic material, or a kind of consumption. The 'snake-like' extremities of the Breeder phyle are clearly intended to invoke the tentacle rape trope of certain anime/manga, but the intention is so different from the human notion of rape as to be effectively separate in my mind. Now that it's been done once, the Breeders may not even be interested in additional genetic material - they did get a whole airlock full of multinational genetic samples after all and the second contact was (suspiciously) much more genial.<br /> Just to be clear, i'm extremely uncomfortable role-playing through intimate encounters in a tabletop setting to begin with, i could never utilize a threat of rape in-game. But i also don't think that's what the PC vs Coalition encounter is meant to be, it's just a really nasty bit of xeno-diplomacy gone wrong and in my opinion an appropriately alien result. It benefits from the animal response it provokes.<br /> Really, it's your decision in the end, always. But missing out on the majesty of the Coalition's final approach at FTL because of the first contact 'ick' factor (understatement, i know) is a sad consequence indeed - the 'ick' just makes it that much more primal. I believe the 'what we know' document promises a trinity with '100% less rape', which is nice - but i honestly think a little something could be lost without at least the allusion for the Coalition first contact. I don't know, maybe i'm just sick in the head...<br /> -shrug-<br /> Just one (sick in the head) posters two cents.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 7. I didn't, sadly, which could have been interesting in a number of ways. In light of what the characters now knew about their family lines, what could happen with another incident? They presumably have other relatives; 200+ years after the start of it all, they probably have some they've never even met. Do they run home and check on their relatives? If a family member becomes an aberrant, how do they respond? Do they even know if the fresh aberrant they're hunting is family? It varies. </div> </div> </div>It certainly puts an interesting question mark at the end of the meta-plot. The event figured prominently into my thinking about Mercer, but if i have any unfulfilled desires in the 2120 setting, it would be to run a slightly pulpy 2123+ Coalition invasion. With Eden, the Colony, Aliens including the Doyen, giant VARGs, epic space battles and a shit-load of dramatic editing for all - hell throw in some time travellers and whatever else is lying about too. Bring in the new inspired with a bang.<br /> For you it would have made a more complex tool, as you've detailed - pity you didn't get there, would have been a thematically good time to execute the cyclical conclusion.<br /> <br /> Thanks for sharing this Drazosh - it sounds like it was so much fun that i'm smiling just thinking about it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4458,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4458-nihilist","user":"Nihilist","date":".  I need to learn how to spell... 04-28-2015, 06:21 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4458-nihilist","name":"Nihilist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4458_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-28T15:50:27-07:00","id":495326,"title":"","content":"Thank you! I'm just glad for the opportunity to share and relive it a bit. Like I said, it was a blast. Ask as many questions as you like.<br /> <br /> 1. They never interacted directly again, although it was implied that Max wanted to try to talk him out of his path. One of my ideas for the fallen T2M member, however, was to have him drop the troupe in Divis Mal's nova dimension and die in the process. A reality where each nova was a god in his own world under the supreme Donighal would have been fun to play out.<br /> 2. I deliberately left the bulk of his age and life vague, although he did appear at different stages of his life every time they saw him. This happened in Adventure! as well, but the lack of context mostly just left it a mystery until later periods. When they realized it was him in Aberrant, it became clear.<br /> 3. Outside of Adventure!, I used daredevils in as subtle a manner as possible. There were several throughout all eras of the campaign, but it was never explicitly stated; most of them were even unaware that they were special at all.<br /> 3.5. In truth, I have a touch of hate for d20 as a whole, and they never gave that much detail on the descendants of Mercer (making them excellent NPC fodder, I think).<br /> 4. I love using Max as a story guide who can't directly affect as much as he'd like and is forced to use subtle hints and warnings to direct people so that they can do the most good. This keeps him from overshadowing the PCs while still keeping gaming groups (especially those without much RPG experience, like my group at the time) on track.<br /> 5. To a degree, I can't blame them for mistrusting Proteus. Even when their goals and methods are more objectively good, they are still difficult to really trust.<br /> 6. I felt the same way. In my opinion, one of the Trinity Universe's greatest strengths is the vast array of NPCs and setting details; they really make you feel like you're in a living, breathing world instead of just a setting. It can be a challenge, though, to keep the PCs from getting outshined by the likes of Maxwell Mercer, Michael Donighal, Caestus Pax, and the proxies. These characters are powerful, larger than life, but ultimately bound by their fates. The PCs are the ones who are truly dynamic.<br /> 6a. Overall, I agree with you, and their segment of the Alien Invasion series does a great job of filling them out beyond the impression you get in the core book. The fact remains, however, that the core book does an excellent job of making them unapproachable. The word &quot;rape&quot; is even the last one on the page; it's meant to stick with the reader, but it's always felt like a weak and ham-fisted way of provoking horror and outrage in the reader. I am glad that Ian and company are letting them stand on their own depth.<br /> 7. I was looking forward to the event, especially with the image of newly-erupted novas and tankless psions leaping to the party's aid against the Coalition. The reaction of the Doyen (my personal choice for central antagonist of the Trinity/Aeon era) would have been spectacular, and the whole event would have led the way to the mysterious Setting After Aeon hinted at by Ian and co.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4732,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4732-drazosh","user":"Drazosh","date":".  Curse typing on an iPad. 04-28-2015, 06:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4732-drazosh","name":"Drazosh","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"50"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-29T01:43:58-07:00","id":496092,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Drazosh</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-\u00e6on/494207-my-aeon-marathon?p=495326#post495326\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thank you! I'm just glad for the opportunity to share and relive it a bit. Like I said, it was a blast. Ask as many questions as you like.<br /> <br /> 1. They never interacted directly again, although it was implied that Max wanted to try to talk him out of his path. One of my ideas for the fallen T2M member, however, was to have him drop the troupe in Divis Mal's nova dimension and die in the process. A reality where each nova was a god in his own world under the supreme Donighal would have been fun to play out.</div> </div> </div> </div>Pity, the battle with Primoris felt a bit underwhelming from a narrative standpoint, but i can also see how it's big enough to cap an era with. I like the faded T2M allusion, but where would you have gone with a game leaving the known universe? How would 2120 Psions have processed that, and why would the aberrant who dropped them there die? Just for the drama, or some other reason?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 2. I deliberately left the bulk of his age and life vague, although he did appear at different stages of his life every time they saw him. This happened in Adventure! as well, but the lack of context mostly just left it a mystery until later periods. When they realized it was him in Aberrant, it became clear. </div> </div> </div>Sounds like the safe thing to do. Fitting time travel onto a pre-written setting is tough work - did you ever really need to make a call on how many Aeon society members knew about their founders temporal travels?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3. Outside of Adventure!, I used daredevils in as subtle a manner as possible. There were several throughout all eras of the campaign, but it was never explicitly stated; most of them were even unaware that they were special at all. </div> </div> </div>Nice, subtle as intended. But you never had to run Daredevil rules against the Novas or Psions from later settings? How did you handle dramatic editing in Adventure!? What kinds of pulp themes did you try out while you were playing?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3.5. In truth, I have a touch of hate for d20 as a whole, and they never gave that much detail on the descendants of Mercer (making them excellent NPC fodder, I think). </div> </div> </div>I found the d20 release of Trinity strangely unappealing given my level of enthusiasm for the setting getting a second run - i never really figured out why, because i never felt obliged enough to actually purchase them. So i can't speak to the quality of the books with any authority - only my own ambivalence. Still, Margaret is a helpful element to glue Adventure! to Aberrant at least. How did she relate to Thetis, if you know?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 4. I love using Max as a story guide who can't directly affect as much as he'd like and is forced to use subtle hints and warnings to direct people so that they can do the most good. This keeps him from overshadowing the PCs while still keeping gaming groups (especially those without much RPG experience, like my group at the time) on track. </div> </div> </div>My brother used Max like that a couple of times or more, we liked to use a light hearted approach to the original Aeon Society, so the encounters would be fun (if sometimes a little silly) - but mostly just as background and diversions. I'm not certain how i would approach the Adventure! setting personally, likely more seriously - but still with some element of absurdity.<br /> It sounds like you have a pretty solid grasp of Mercer as an actor, but i wonder if you ever used him for the pure entertainment value of an old pulp adventurer out of his element?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 5. To a degree, I can't blame them for mistrusting Proteus. Even when their goals and methods are more objectively good, they are still difficult to really trust. </div> </div> </div>I know, i felt for them as Novas, when the sentiment carries over into your Darkness Revealed campaign however, things can get sticky. Some mistrust is fine, they are still a dirty spy division of the Trinity - but that mistrust spreading too deep into the other two divisions renders a lot of setting material very dubious. Or more correctly, even more dubious than intended.<br /> -smile-<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 6. I felt the same way. In my opinion, one of the Trinity Universe's greatest strengths is the vast array of NPCs and setting details; they really make you feel like you're in a living, breathing world instead of just a setting. It can be a challenge, though, to keep the PCs from getting outshined by the likes of Maxwell Mercer, Michael Donighal, Caestus Pax, and the proxies. These characters are powerful, larger than life, but ultimately bound by their fates. The PCs are the ones who are truly dynamic. </div> </div> </div>You are preaching to the choir, i love the meta-plot, but the PCs are the drivers in the end. Having developed and realized NPCs populating the setting helps greatly - but they aren't running the show. The most leeway i give NPCs for world alteration are the immediate and dynamic villains story-to-story - anything else is background story. Although the background story should be careful not to eclipse the players either.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 6a. Overall, I agree with you, and their segment of the Alien Invasion series does a great job of filling them out beyond the impression you get in the core book. The fact remains, however, that the core book does an excellent job of making them unapproachable. The word &quot;rape&quot; is even the last one on the page; it's meant to stick with the reader, but it's always felt like a weak and ham-fisted way of provoking horror and outrage in the reader. I am glad that Ian and company are letting them stand on their own depth. </div> </div> </div>Yeah, i think i could do without rubbing the rape allusion in now that you make it so clear, i think the comparison is pretty clear when human-phyles show up. The genetic ravaging of the galaxy however is alien enough that i'd like it to stay in is all. But i don't see why you weren't able to just bypass that introduction for something else - maybe write up something yourself with some minor changes? Too much time spent of the actual game, i guess?<br /> -shrug-<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 7. I was looking forward to the event, especially with the image of newly-erupted novas and tankless psions leaping to the party's aid against the Coalition. The reaction of the Doyen (my personal choice for central antagonist of the Trinity/Aeon era) would have been spectacular, and the whole event would have led the way to the mysterious Setting After Aeon hinted at by Ian and co. </div> </div> </div>I may be a bit anxious about the new team moving the Aeon/Trinity start date forward a smidgeon, but the incoming Ark was a great cliffhanger to leave us with and it should be interesting to see where Onyx Path goes with that narrative momentum. You also singled out the Doyen? If you were lurking often, you might also know this is my own leaning. Care to give a list of things you were willing to lay at whatever the Doyen have instead of feet? Maybe i missed some i could use myself.<br /> -smile-<br /> I think Ian W. might have been talking about a setting long after the Trinity years - a full scale, galactic, space opera - if i'm not mistaken. But i claim no expertise...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4458-nihilist","name":"Nihilist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4458_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-29T16:46:20-07:00","id":496876,"title":"","content":"1. While I agree it was a poor conclusion for their interactions, I feel there's something pulpy about conclusions that are somewhat hollow. As an example (perhaps chronologically incorrect, but arguably correct thematically), consider the case of Sherlock Holmes and Professor Moriarty. The two nemeses likely deserved a resolution to their rivalry but, in the end, each could only finally defeat the other at the cost of his own life. The battle of '43 was, to a degree, Reichenbach for Mercer and Donighal; they might never prove whose ideology was &quot;correct,&quot; but their paths would never be the same again.<br /> As for the lost aberrant, while I had deliberately left it vague, I described it similarly to an old sidekick of their novas, a relatively weak nova (if such a thing were possible) designed to ensure their humility by obligating them to care for another of their kind. His only power was Warp, and I usually played him as bungling it on a regular basis. For him to use that much power, it would have been a sacrifice indeed.<br /> 2. I never had to make that call directly, but I tried to keep it to Donighal, the PCs, and the highest ranks of Aeon wherever possible.<br /> 3. There was one instance where I actually rolled daredevil abilities, and I pretty much played it straight (versus novas, it wasn't balanced, but it wasn't meant to be). Dramatic editing, sadly, did not come up much; my players weren't totally comfortable with the idea at the time, so they avoided it for the most part.<br /> 3.5. I don't know how the books handled her, off the top of my head. If I recall correctly, however, Thetis was genetically predisposed to becoming a nova, but never actually erupted.<br /> 4. Outside of the Adventure! Era, and even during it when the scene was less... Well, adventurous... I definitely played him as a fish out of water. The players and their characters commented on his lack of familiarity with &quot;common&quot; technology and concepts. Adventure!, I felt, was at its best when it was played a bit on the camp side. Pulp era stories always seem that way to modern sensibilities, so why not play it up?<br /> 5. When you are &quot;in the know&quot; about Proteus in the Aberrant setting, it's hard to see them in any other light. To my mind, though, that is their cross to bear, and one they must bear in order to do what needs to be done in 2120+.<br /> 6. We agree completely, there.<br /> 6a. I had planned on using them after the lone aberrant meeting and however that was to be resolved, but never got the chance. The rape bit would have been left out, however. It just wasn't needed to make them a compelling antagonist.<br /> 7. To be honest, I started my game near that start date (I left the Upeo wa Macho as an option for the players, although they did not take it, but I left much of the order still in Chromatic custody so that I could run that arc), so it does not hurt my feelings much. I had introduced the players to the Doyen earlier, with one that was in Nippon and getting closer to that country's secrets, but that final scene in the Chromatic invasion really makes you hate them, which I needed. Truthfully, I blamed them for most of the Trinity-centric problems save the aberrants, Huang-Marr, and the FSA (the evil that men do...). As I've said previously, they are selfish, paranoid, and manipulative; even the pro-human faction is only thus for their own petty and self-serving reasons. For a game line about coming together and using incredible powers for the good of humanity (and later, its allies), that kind of species should seem almost more evil than the aberrants themselves. They use sentient beings as tools for their own security, and are very much the antithesis of what Aeon stands for.<br /> That said, I think their influence will need to go for humanity to truly progress past where they are. The pro-human Doyen need human psions to remain ignorant, driving themselves into the aberrants and all other quantum-manipulators. The human race cannot reach for the stars and form a civilization (which is what I've heard as well, and I had toyed with the idea as a sort of Adventure!, all three character types working together again, sort of bookend) beyond its limited expansion in Trinity while the Doyen of either faction are still there to keep humanity under their mind-boots, I feel.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4732-drazosh","name":"Drazosh","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"50"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-30T01:42:27-07:00","id":497235,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Drazosh</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-\u00e6on/494207-my-aeon-marathon?p=496876#post496876\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1. While I agree it was a poor conclusion for their interactions, I feel there's something pulpy about conclusions that are somewhat hollow. As an example (perhaps chronologically incorrect, but arguably correct thematically), consider the case of Sherlock Holmes and Professor Moriarty. The two nemeses likely deserved a resolution to their rivalry but, in the end, each could only finally defeat the other at the cost of his own life. The battle of '43 was, to a degree, Reichenbach for Mercer and Donighal; they might never prove whose ideology was &quot;correct,&quot; but their paths would never be the same again.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's admittedly a good point, early pulp was often left open by writers who didn't know whether the serialization would continue. I always tended to view Adventure! as a modern pulp-adventure with some lip-service throwbacks - so modern narrative structures over the trappings of the golden age of comics or serial digests of the early 20th century. But going full-on throwback in regards to Mercer and Donighal does fit the era theme, no doubt. But that arguement also lends weight to the 'recurring villain' trope of superhero comics that suggested continuation of Mercer v Donighal in the Aberrant years - because the new theme now lends itself to that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As for the lost aberrant, while I had deliberately left it vague, I described it similarly to an old sidekick of their novas, a relatively weak nova (if such a thing were possible) designed to ensure their humility by obligating them to care for another of their kind. His only power was Warp, and I usually played him as bungling it on a regular basis. For him to use that much power, it would have been a sacrifice indeed. </div> </div> </div>Intriguing, a haunting reminder to players out of character. Did you present any other 2120 aberrants in a potentially sympathetic light? Or am i entirely misreading this? What was your take on the Colony and by extension, Kali and Yog-Death, his only named co-conspirators?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 2. I never had to make that call directly, but I tried to keep it to Donighal, the PCs, and the highest ranks of Aeon wherever possible. </div> </div> </div>Secrets will almost inevitably escape from numbers greater than one - so the circle in the know needs to remain small if the truth must remain hidden. I ran it as being a rite of passage type discovery known to elite Aeon agents (such as Hector Ramirez) who had done the research and reading needed, like a kind of scavenger hunt appreciated by investigative Daredevils. The higher end members all know, but don't share out of respect for the game - if it were ever to get in the way of something more important, any decent agent would spill the beans - because despite that revelation - only Mercer himself really knows how it works. Although i think Donighal likely has it figured out enough - maybe even enough to be waiting for him in 2061, if he hasn't left earth by then.<br /> -muses-<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3. There was one instance where I actually rolled daredevil abilities, and I pretty much played it straight (versus novas, it wasn't balanced, but it wasn't meant to be). Dramatic editing, sadly, did not come up much; my players weren't totally comfortable with the idea at the time, so they avoided it for the most part.<br /> 3.5. I don't know how the books handled her, off the top of my head. If I recall correctly, however, Thetis was genetically predisposed to becoming a nova, but never actually erupted. </div> </div> </div>What circumstance had you running daredevils against novas in the 2008 setting (if not the exact year)? The cross-over of Daredevil and Nova/Psion was interesting to me because i had a pet theory that Daredevils/Paramorphs/Talents could become inspired from interacting with other active inspired. I found dramatic editing needed some situating too - i'm so used to playing from how the die fall that in my one inspiration powered character - i was having too much fun role-playing the character that i never really thought of changing things in my favor. When i thought of ways to do it, i generally found the in game solution more appealing than my own deus ex machina (i don't get to play as often as i'd like anyway, mostly ST).<br /> -shrug-<br /> Pity, because my brother uses it well when he played inspired (he works in his character theme and even ST descriptions when he remembers) and it never really broke the game - so as a mechanism it seemed fun.<br /> Well, it was worth asking - having never read the material or talked to anyone who has - i really have no idea about Margaret Mercer, except that she exists.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 4. Outside of the Adventure! Era, and even during it when the scene was less... Well, adventurous... I definitely played him as a fish out of water. The players and their characters commented on his lack of familiarity with &quot;common&quot; technology and concepts. Adventure!, I felt, was at its best when it was played a bit on the camp side. Pulp era stories always seem that way to modern sensibilities, so why not play it up? </div> </div> </div>I'd imagine even trying to do lovecraftian pulp-horror in adventure could lead to some relatively light hearted play - with super science and dramatic editing aligned to frustrate and combat the unspeakable, eldritch terrors against them. When everything comes together in pulp, you no longer see the awkward details and instead only see the romance of adventure, the clinch of suspense and the daring in your own heart.<br /> Even the League of Extraordinary Gentlemen is prone to a lampshade moment here and there to revel in the absurdity of it all - and it tends to play things pretty straight when it isn't deconstructing its own concept. A lot of Warren Ellis' Planetary also plays a similar genre pretty straight - so those stories are there and i would delve into that deeper in a dedicated Adventure! or post Venezuela event game.<br /> But i'd hate to ignore the silly fun to be had in the genre - it would be an injustice.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 5. When you are &quot;in the know&quot; about Proteus in the Aberrant setting, it's hard to see them in any other light. To my mind, though, that is their cross to bear, and one they must bear in order to do what needs to be done in 2120+. </div> </div> </div>This is true, there is a sense of remorse in Proteus and the Aeon Trinity as a whole in the 2120 material - just not enough to compensate without any real narrative counter-weight to those darkest days of sterilization, vivisection and rape. I had no issue at all with the characterization that lead to these trust issues, i found him one of the most engaging i'd ever run in 2120 - it was just a lot of work convincing the character to trust the whole ediface not to come pouring down onto him every time they sent a new mission directive or lead out of the DR material.<br /> Eventually, i found compromise in redirecting all loose blame at the Psi Orders, who represent memetic/noetic Doyen strongholds anyway - a new way to blame the Doyen, but indirectly. The material at least supported that approach.<br /> -smile-<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 7. To be honest, I started my game near that start date (I left the Upeo wa Macho as an option for the players, although they did not take it, but I left much of the order still in Chromatic custody so that I could run that arc), so it does not hurt my feelings much. </div> </div> </div>So, i'm curious if you ran the Huang-Marr episodes of DR? It sounds like you ran the Chromatic Invasion from those books at least.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I had introduced the players to the Doyen earlier, with one that was in Nippon and getting closer to that country's secrets, but that final scene in the Chromatic invasion really makes you hate them, which I needed. Truthfully, I blamed them for most of the Trinity-centric problems save the aberrants, Huang-Marr, and the FSA (the evil that men do...). As I've said previously, they are selfish, paranoid, and manipulative; even the pro-human faction is only thus for their own petty and self-serving reasons. For a game line about coming together and using incredible powers for the good of humanity (and later, its allies), that kind of species should seem almost more evil than the aberrants themselves. They use sentient beings as tools for their own security, and are very much the antithesis of what Aeon stands for. </div> </div> </div>Good thinking about the Doyen investigating Nippon, i ran a low-quantum nova game out of 2120 Nippon and i would have used that idea myself if i'd gotten the time to build to it.<br /> I think you and i are going to be allies against the Doyen invasion around here Drazosh.<br /> -brotherly handshake-<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That said, I think their influence will need to go for humanity to truly progress past where they are. The pro-human Doyen need human psions to remain ignorant, driving themselves into the aberrants and all other quantum-manipulators. The human race cannot reach for the stars and form a civilization (which is what I've heard as well, and I had toyed with the idea as a sort of Adventure!, all three character types working together again, sort of bookend) beyond its limited expansion in Trinity while the Doyen of either faction are still there to keep humanity under their mind-boots, I feel. </div> </div> </div>Hence, why the Psi Orders as they are cannot stand for long - at least not in my hope for the setting. I've hinted before that i might have built toward a anti-doyen kind of species with Quantum leanings being encountered at the far end of Mgitu's Zepp-Gate (but only very subtly at first) - which could have turned into a kind of galactic war vaguely reminiscent of Babylon 5, i would have worked the Coalition invasion into that too...<br /> -muses-<br /> What, in your opinion, were the Doyen anyway? What were the important differences in the pro-human and anti-human factions? What do you think their end game was?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4458-nihilist","name":"Nihilist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4458_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-30T18:25:57-07:00","id":498066,"title":"","content":"1. It would have been fun to go into their conflict a bit further, but alas, it was not to be. If I ever get the pleasure of running another marathon (perhaps with the new editions; I loved the games despite their warts, but cleaner systems and more coherent settings would be appreciated).<br /> As for the aberrants, I made every effort to make them minorly sympathetic. For all their power and insanity, they were human once; players should never be permitted to forget that.<br /> 2. To a degree, I almost think that's why Divis Mal stepped into another dimension with his followers. When you are a godlike being and there is only one man who can hurt you (emotionally, at least), and that man can follow you across time and, eventually, space, how else can you find peace?<br /> 3. Mostly, the one that stands out in my memory was a cat burglar with unnatural skill who was hired to steal nova scientific developments from Project Utopia. They managed to catch him, but it took some effort.<br /> 4. Pulp can certainly be serious, even dark, but I don't really feel it fits Adventure in my point of view. There is no badwrongfun, and I may lose some geek cred on this, but I'm not actually a fan of Lovecraftian horror, particularly in this world. They call it the World of Brightness for a reason; bad things might happen, but the characters can and should always be able to overcome it. Hope - Sacrifice - Unity, and never, ever despair and futility.<br /> 5. Proteus, even without their dark history, may never fully cleanse themselves of the stigma of being the black ops group in a society built on guiding humanity to greater brilliance.<br /> I'll cover the Doyen and Orders later on.<br /> 7. My players were busy during Huang-Marr, as I didn't feel like it would have suited the themes I was running with too much, but they did hear about it, and our Rex had to deal with the fallout. Mostly, it was news headlines and some ugly looks that only cleared up after the Chromatic invasion. I also ran the Qin house conflict, simply because I absolutely adore the Qin.<br /> As for the Doyen... To continue the Babylon 5 reference, I saw them similar to Vorlons. Ancient beyond understanding, but superior and manipulative. There was no Kosh Naranek; the Doyen do what they do because of obsessive self-interest, which is part of why their species has lived so long. The anti-human faction sees humanity as a constant threat; they have the potential to manipulate quantum, and these noetic beings are, quite simply, terrified. The pro-human faction, I never saw as sincerely pro-human, just pragmatic. Why fight a war against a horrifying opponent when you can get it to destroy itself with minimal effort? They played on humanity's fear and anger over the atrocities of the Aberrant War, gave them a little boost, and let the sparks fly. In the end, both factions would have happily seen humanity extinguished to ensure that no more quantum manipulators (or, given their natural capacity to evolve beyond expectations, noetics not under their control) rose from that species to threaten them again; the only difference between the factions, I believe, is in their methods.<br /> Venezuela, then, would have been a nightmare scenario for both sides. They named their proxies wishfully, and one most likely tried to shake his yoke. The race uplifted by the anti-human faction to put humanity down has not only failed, but most likely allied itself with the humans, and one of the Doyen responsible quite foolishly revealed himself to everyone involved. Worse, more of humanity found itself with powers that the Doyen did not grant. The situation has properly fallen out of their control. As Vermillion can attest, the Doyen do not react well when things have fallen out of their control.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4732-drazosh","name":"Drazosh","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"50"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-02T01:50:24-07:00","id":499992,"title":"","content":"Apologies for the delay. Thanks for your patience and the great replies, Drazosh.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Drazosh</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-\u00e6on/494207-my-aeon-marathon?p=498066#post498066\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1. It would have been fun to go into their conflict a bit further, but alas, it was not to be. If I ever get the pleasure of running another marathon (perhaps with the new editions; I loved the games despite their warts, but cleaner systems and more coherent settings would be appreciated).<br /> As for the aberrants, I made every effort to make them minorly sympathetic. For all their power and insanity, they were human once; players should never be permitted to forget that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Perhaps it's something that OPP will have an opportunity to address. I'm certain Mercer &amp; Donighal would have figured prominently into an extended run for Adventure at least. To steal a sentiment i first heard from CHILL: even if i have to discard the whole thing and go back to the storyteller books, OPP's edition will still be an interesting read. Since you have the sympathetic approach to 2120 Aberrants, i would love to hear how you handled the Colony, Anders Nash and any other taint-mad, named aberrants who get 'screen time' in the series? How did the taint manifest for the ones you ran, what depictions influenced your vision, how did they relate to humanity and each other, etc...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 2. To a degree, I almost think that's why Divis Mal stepped into another dimension with his followers. When you are a godlike being and there is only one man who can hurt you (emotionally, at least), and that man can follow you across time and, eventually, space, how else can you find peace? </div> </div> </div>I suppose my own head-canon has polluted the water there - as my Mercer couldn't travel past the Venezuela event, which Donighal would know i assumed. I wasn't a big fan of putting Mal on the shelf in that way - don't get me wrong, he needs to be out of the 2120s hair, but universe egress just seemed a little hand-wavey to me.<br /> But i can follow and sympathize with your presentation as well.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3. Mostly, the one that stands out in my memory was a cat burglar with unnatural skill who was hired to steal nova scientific developments from Project Utopia. They managed to catch him, but it took some effort. </div> </div> </div>Sounds like a fun cross-over, i approve. I stumbled across the notion of Daredevils being successful against 1998+ Novas when they were willing to make the ultimate sacrifice in the process - but i only ever used it as a narrative device, never worked out systems for it - seems more like DE than a knack anyway.<br /> I got some use out of Nippon's superiors, a 'villain' with access to whatever Dramatic Editing is offscreen can be a pretty loaded foe - even for powerhouses like Novas.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 4. Pulp can certainly be serious, even dark, but I don't really feel it fits Adventure in my point of view. There is no badwrongfun, and I may lose some geek cred on this, but I'm not actually a fan of Lovecraftian horror, particularly in this world. They call it the World of Brightness for a reason; bad things might happen, but the characters can and should always be able to overcome it. Hope - Sacrifice - Unity, and never, ever despair and futility. </div> </div> </div>We might have to agree to disagree on that point - i can't go without dark shades in the setting, but i wouldn't be comfortable excising the absurd to accomodate my perspective alone either. A strength i keep getting pulled back to is how well the triple setting can tackle any given genre.<br /> I used Lovecraft because of how poorly that genre-style would mesh, despite the similarities in genre (exploration, mystery, archaeology, etc...). Call of Cthulhu informs my vision of 1920s gaming, forgive the reference. An Aeon Society retelling of a classic CoC scenario like the Haunting (but preferably something with a less memorable reveal, for better mystery) would've beeen high on my priorities had i ever gotten around to running Adventure! - because so many of my players know that scenario and would likely revel in the chance to revisit it with pulp heroes. One of the old 5th ed. CoC books had an adventure where you travel to the Jurrassic to fight dinosaurs and elder things - you can't tell me that isn't an Aeon Society serial just waiting to be written. Lovecraft even wrote an inspired escape from the undead infested underworld in Rats in the Walls (iirc) himself, so there are common threads at least.<br /> 'They' may call it that, but i think you might find it hard to find me referring to 'world of brightness' outside of inverted commas or quote marks. I get that it's about there being hope for a better tomorrow - but without the contrasting 'world of darkness' the term is a bit misleading i think. The setting is even big enough for some despair and futility - making Mercer even more heroic for facing down the dark future and never relinquishing hope.<br /> But, we can disagree too - like i said; it's big enough for that at least.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 5. Proteus, even without their dark history, may never fully cleanse themselves of the stigma of being the black ops group in a society built on guiding humanity to greater brilliance. </div> </div> </div>True. Maybe i'm conflating this conversation and another i had a while ago about Proteus, where the black mark against them seemed too black to forgive. I have no issue with Proteus from 2120, i don't know the Proteus from 2008 as well and i've been told they do some horrendous things i've never read myself.<br /> -shrug-<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 7. My players were busy during Huang-Marr, as I didn't feel like it would have suited the themes I was running with too much, but they did hear about it, and our Rex had to deal with the fallout. Mostly, it was news headlines and some ugly looks that only cleared up after the Chromatic invasion. I also ran the Qin house conflict, simply because I absolutely adore the Qin. </div> </div> </div>Huang-Marr is essential Trinity for my groups, if that helps to illustrate our differences on the theme there - but it also made the PC i keep mentioning in regards to not trusting Proteus get positively maniacal with paranoia. So, maybe i could have done that myself. The Alien invasion/encounter adventures are generally the ones i look forward to more, but Huang-Marr was important establishment of the settings mood too - at least in my opinion.<br /> You've outlined the stories you used instead, but could you elaborate a bit, to get a sense of how you set the intended mood for the period?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As for the Doyen... To continue the Babylon 5 reference, I saw them similar to Vorlons. Ancient beyond understanding, but superior and manipulative. There was no Kosh Naranek; the Doyen do what they do because of obsessive self-interest, which is part of why their species has lived so long. The anti-human faction sees humanity as a constant threat; they have the potential to manipulate quantum, and these noetic beings are, quite simply, terrified. The pro-human faction, I never saw as sincerely pro-human, just pragmatic. Why fight a war against a horrifying opponent when you can get it to destroy itself with minimal effort? They played on humanity's fear and anger over the atrocities of the Aberrant War, gave them a little boost, and let the sparks fly. In the end, both factions would have happily seen humanity extinguished to ensure that no more quantum manipulators (or, given their natural capacity to evolve beyond expectations, noetics not under their control) rose from that species to threaten them again; the only difference between the factions, I believe, is in their methods.<br /> Venezuela, then, would have been a nightmare scenario for both sides. They named their proxies wishfully, and one most likely tried to shake his yoke. The race uplifted by the anti-human faction to put humanity down has not only failed, but most likely allied itself with the humans, and one of the Doyen responsible quite foolishly revealed himself to everyone involved. Worse, more of humanity found itself with powers that the Doyen did not grant. The situation has properly fallen out of their control. As Vermillion can attest, the Doyen do not react well when things have fallen out of their control. </div> </div> </div>This is close to what i had picked up too, i would have to re-read the Doyen appearance and check my references again, but that sounds like it would fly for me. Without doing the minimal look-up required to check, i seem to remember the Trinity series was coming out about the same time as Babylon 5. So i doubt the Vorlons escaped the original designers attention at the time, i don't know if they were doing the galactic war at that stage yet. Maybe there was a Doyen Kosh out there, maybe one of the Proxies had the hook-up with a more moderate Doyen of some value?<br /> We would have to make an arbitrary call on what they were as a species first, how they perceived three dimensional space, their evolutionary imperatives, their relations to one another and the rest of the universe, that kind of stuff. So i don't claim they can't be rescued from their villainy, but i can't see how you can look at the material presented about the Doyen and not be suspicious/concerned. I'm glad i've heard from someone else who saw them as potentially worse than the exiled Aberrants of the era.<br /> -smile-<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4458-nihilist","name":"Nihilist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4458_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-02T22:14:15-07:00","id":501367,"title":"","content":"1. I actually didn't use the Colony directly, simply because his characterization was almost too unsympathetic to fit my style. The best, most believable villains, in my view, are the ones who, while thoroughly warped in morality and outlook, are almost sympathetic. You can almost agree with them, even though you know they're wrong. The Colony is just a monster, and while there's certainly room for him, and I can't say I'd never use him, but he's not as compelling to me as he could be. That said, aberrants are pretty monstrous, and most of them have been twisted utterly by a need for revenge and to be worshipped and feared. How they express that colors the way the taint manipulates them; an aberrant who sees himself as a destroyer of the weak manifested a wrecking ball for an arm, for instance, while another who viewed aberrant kind as parts of a unified whole developed insectoid features. I seldom plan games out in the traditional sense, mostly just keepig stats and concepts in my head, but I tried to build each one as a nova and then twisted it into the worst case, least human scenario. The human core, however, was something I always strived to preserve.<br /> And yes, I really don't care if I have to go back to storyteller after all is said and done; I still won't regret buying the new editions. That said, OPP has done nothing but impress me thus far, so I remain optimistic.<br /> 2. I'm not sure if Mercer could travel past 418, but I'm not sure that Mal was even aware that it was coming. He himself left before that time came. That said, I'm not a fan of Mal creating his own sandbox and fleeing for good, which is why I wanted to take the group there. His story needed a better conclusion, following &quot;Your legacy is our future,&quot; than just taking his toys and going home. If he wanted to hide outside of space and time, I sure as hell wasn't going to let him.<br /> 3. My feeling on daredevils is that, while novas might have the edge on them in raw power, they are probably the ultimate specialists. They might not shoot quantum bolts, but their abilities carry them through their preferred specialty. My cat burglar couldn't fly or disintegrated matter, but he was action movie good (as much as I hate making the reference, think the fellow from Ocean's 12 or Parker from Leverage) at being a cat burglar, so he was still quite a challenge, since the conflict was on his terms.<br /> 4. I'm okay with disagreement. My views on Lovecraftian horror are unpopular, and I'm perfectly okay with that. There is no badwrongfun, and people are perfectly free to put some existential despair all over their Adventure games. Personally, I just feel that there should be some hope for the future, some way for the heroes to defy the odds, stare Lovecraft in the eye, and sock him in the face if they have the will and tenacity to do so. Will they always succeed? Certainly not. In my games, however, that failure will never be because the world is fated to be consumed. If it provides context, I also avoided the constant Gehenna/whatever-mongering in the classic World of Darkness too.<br /> 5. I don't know that their black mark is unforgivable. I do think, however, that they need to work hard to cleanse the stigma from the minds of those who remember. No one with half a brain would consider modern Germany a fascist country, for example, but they've had to work hard to erase the stigma.<br /> 7. I loved Huang-Marr, and I'll probably run it in a later game. It just didn't feel right for this one, if that makes sense.<br /> <br /> There were a few different stories, and I don't have complete notes for all of them, but I can go over the first assignment of that period. Orgotek requested some help investigating odd FSA movements near their New York arcology, including giving several class-1 citizens trips to locations of their choice &quot;in appreciation for their patriotism.&quot; They did not think it was particularly serious, but they wanted a (mostly) unaffiliated team to have a look without giving Orgotek bureaucratic grief. The investigation led them along some false leads, including the (to me) standard FSA excuse of &quot;terrorist activity in the area.&quot; In the end, however, they determined that it was quite serious; the FSA was preparing to detonate the generators and reactors for the arcologies nearest to Orgotek. That kind of sudden, violent death on such a scale, in attunement range, would have theoretically knocked Orgotek's psions off their feet long enough for a sizable military force to drop the hammer. Of course, Orgotek and Cassel are not particularly quiet about their distaste for the fascist state, and they most likely support groups who might bring it down. The group had to discover the attack, foil the mass murder of innocent people in the process, and diffuse the situation without antagonizing the FSA any further. In the end, they wound up calling attention to the explosives in the global media, and the FSA was forced to laud them as heroes who foiled a terrorist attack. Aeon smoothed ruffled feathers, with the typical mix of diplomacy and veiled threats, but the group was still less than welcome on American soil after that.<br /> <br /> As for the Doyen, I'd actually like to see a Kosh out of them, even though we saw how that went for Kosh. I'm a fan of dissenting voices. Physically, they were described as balls of light and plasma, almost like miniature suns (part of why it was so easy for them to manipulate the Chromatics; they already worshipped Gods of Light, so physical embodiments of light claiming to speak for the gods had an easy time of it). How they looked before? Who knows? I hope to find out in the future, though. To my mind, they're the central villains of the era. The aberrants are nightmares and painful reminders of the past, but the games are very much about improving the future. The Doyen seemed dead-set on controlling that future, one way or the other.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4732-drazosh","name":"Drazosh","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"50"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-04T20:56:26-07:00","id":503766,"title":"","content":"Apologies again for my delay.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Drazosh</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-\u00e6on/494207-my-aeon-marathon?p=501367#post501367\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1. I actually didn't use the Colony directly, simply because his characterization was almost too unsympathetic to fit my style. The best, most believable villains, in my view, are the ones who, while thoroughly warped in morality and outlook, are almost sympathetic. You can almost agree with them, even though you know they're wrong. The Colony is just a monster, and while there's certainly room for him, and I can't say I'd never use him, but he's not as compelling to me as he could be.</div> </div> </div> </div>Through strange aeons, I found myself in the position where i had to seriously consider the Colonys motivations and historical trajectory - and found nothing in the source material. So, i'd agree that next to the human and alien antagonists of the era, the Colony lacked playability as anything beyond a monolithic 'evil' - which i thought the setting above for the most part. The return is wonderful and potentially chilling, but never really gets enough attention to capitalize on the perilous confrontation.<br /> There is certainly room for the alien-minded, monstrous Aberrants in my game - but as the named representative of so many similar monsters - the Colony could really have used some characterization. Still, you seem to have a vision of the elements you've spoken about, so it was worth asking.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That said, aberrants are pretty monstrous, and most of them have been twisted utterly by a need for revenge and to be worshipped and feared. How they express that colors the way the taint manipulates them; an aberrant who sees himself as a destroyer of the weak manifested a wrecking ball for an arm, for instance, while another who viewed aberrant kind as parts of a unified whole developed insectoid features. I seldom plan games out in the traditional sense, mostly just keepig stats and concepts in my head, but I tried to build each one as a nova and then twisted it into the worst case, least human scenario. The human core, however, was something I always strived to preserve. </div> </div> </div>I like the notion of creating the Nova and then twisting it into an aberrant - i did something similar. I didn't truck with powersets/animas being wish-fulfillment or consciously developed in any way - because i'd (perhaps foolishly) written genetic anima similarities into my own vision of 2nd and 3rd generation Novas.<br /> But, that's my albatross to bear - your version sounds just fine too - i could do it either way and the immediate differences wouldn't be obvious to players. Interesting to compare how another ST handled sympathetic Aberrants in 2120, thanks for taking the time to elaborate for me.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 2. I'm not sure if Mercer could travel past 418, but I'm not sure that Mal was even aware that it was coming. He himself left before that time came. </div> </div> </div>I had assumed that Primoris reasoned it out given Mercer's allusions and Donighal's own unparalleled mental acuity. But had he not, my thinking would have been forced down a different path due to causality.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That said, I'm not a fan of Mal creating his own sandbox and fleeing for good, which is why I wanted to take the group there. His story needed a better conclusion, following &quot;Your legacy is our future,&quot; than just taking his toys and going home. If he wanted to hide outside of space and time, I sure as hell wasn't going to let him. </div> </div> </div>Very nice, i might have followed this example myself, but i was trying to do pretty hard-science and had no idea how to properly ensconce and jargonize dimensional travel into the 2120 technology at the time. I tried to be sure that nearly every expanded Aberrant i characterized had a different post-2061 story of Mal and his fate, leaving the whole mystery entirely unsolved. I treated this particular 'word of god' with more than a pinch of heresy, it just seemed a waste.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3. My feeling on daredevils is that, while novas might have the edge on them in raw power, they are probably the ultimate specialists. They might not shoot quantum bolts, but their abilities carry them through their preferred specialty. My cat burglar couldn't fly or disintegrated matter, but he was action movie good (as much as I hate making the reference, think the fellow from Ocean's 12 or Parker from Leverage) at being a cat burglar, so he was still quite a challenge, since the conflict was on his terms. </div> </div> </div>I'd say we share that approach, at least superficially. The daredevil my brother had squaring off against a nova was a fighting specialist (wuxia style grandmaster sifu) who never actually either managed or bothered to inflict damage, but could dodge all day long.<br /> I'd also like to hear your perspective on Dramatic editing, whether it was something done without conscious awareness or something that even the recipients could tell was an unlikely sequence of events. Were your daredevils self-aware inhabitants of their genre, or just that damn good?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 4. I'm okay with disagreement. My views on Lovecraftian horror are unpopular, and I'm perfectly okay with that. There is no badwrongfun, and people are perfectly free to put some existential despair all over their Adventure games. Personally, I just feel that there should be some hope for the future, some way for the heroes to defy the odds, stare Lovecraft in the eye, and sock him in the face if they have the will and tenacity to do so. Will they always succeed? Certainly not. In my games, however, that failure will never be because the world is fated to be consumed. If it provides context, I also avoided the constant Gehenna/whatever-mongering in the classic World of Darkness too. </div> </div> </div>As i said, i can appreciate where you are coming from - my brother prefers a lighter hearted approach to the setting too. But for all the bright future we're promised, there is a lot of room for despair and darkness in the setting - mostly in the periods between settings (the 2060-70s crash and the 1950-70s waning of inspiration). But even then there are 'bright' things running parallel to the 'dark', which side the group focuses on is entirely up to the individual PC parties involved.<br /> I don't even think we disagree so much as just see the same point from different perspectives. The darker the shadow, the brighter the light and vice versa. So your depiction of the Aeon society as a force for 'good' when averaged out over the years is appealing to me - i just don't want to lose the delicious 'world of greyness' that i've been running - so a pure 'world of brightness' does trigger some revulsion, apologies.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 5. I don't know that their black mark is unforgivable. I do think, however, that they need to work hard to cleanse the stigma from the minds of those who remember. No one with half a brain would consider modern Germany a fascist country, for example, but they've had to work hard to erase the stigma. </div> </div> </div>I've been avoiding this one, because i don't want to do the research into it - sounds a bit sordid by what i've heard actually. Apologies to One Vorlon for quoting him out of context, all due respect.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>One Vorlon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/103062-divis-mal-what-s-his-deal?p=462023#post462023\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">[...] Sure, Aeon is backing Project Utopia . . . .but they're also firmly supporting Project Proteus. And Proteus . . . wow, they're into some seriously evil things. Assassinations and black sites. Kidnapping and torture. Raping female novas, so that Project Proteus scientists can dissect the kids to see what second generation novas will be like. [...]<br /> <br /> (Yep, that's right - the bad guys won the Aberrant War. Hate to disillusion anyone)</div> </div> </div> </div>Asked for a source, One Vorlon provided the following:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>One Vorlon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/103062-divis-mal-what-s-his-deal?p=466665#post466665\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The rape/child vivisection comes up in the <b>Project Utopia</b> book. Its credited to one of the listed Proteus NPCs in the character glossary [...]</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Which i still haven't worked up the courage to look into - but he makes a good point if that source checks out, and i have no reason to suspect it won't. I seem to remember my brother mentioning this before and dismissing it because it mutilates the reader's trust in Aeon, if they still have any left at this stage.<br /> -shrug-<br /> Anyway, a Proteus that sanctioned organized rape is not an organization i can easily forgive. The sterilization and murder? I can swing that, but rape is the special-est little evil in narratives and i don't want to be caught defending the act for any reason. In my (incomplete) perspective, that puts Proteus and a great deal of Aeon's aberrant era efforts beyond the moral event horizon. I realize my whole spiel here is predicated on a source i haven't even checked, so i won't push the issue any further. But how would you vindicate Proteus after that?<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 7. I loved Huang-Marr, and I'll probably run it in a later game. It just didn't feel right for this one, if that makes sense. </div> </div> </div>Apologies if that read as an attack on your appreciation of the setting - it's the furthest intent from my position on the subject. The mood clash is entirely clear once you explained it. Say no more.<br /> -smile-<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There were a few different stories, and I don't have complete notes for all of them, but I can go over the first assignment of that period. Orgotek requested some help investigating odd FSA movements near their New York arcology, including giving several class-1 citizens trips to locations of their choice &quot;in appreciation for their patriotism.&quot; They did not think it was particularly serious, but they wanted a (mostly) unaffiliated team to have a look without giving Orgotek bureaucratic grief. The investigation led them along some false leads, including the (to me) standard FSA excuse of &quot;terrorist activity in the area.&quot; In the end, however, they determined that it was quite serious; the FSA was preparing to detonate the generators and reactors for the arcologies nearest to Orgotek. That kind of sudden, violent death on such a scale, in attunement range, would have theoretically knocked Orgotek's psions off their feet long enough for a sizable military force to drop the hammer. Of course, Orgotek and Cassel are not particularly quiet about their distaste for the fascist state, and they most likely support groups who might bring it down. The group had to discover the attack, foil the mass murder of innocent people in the process, and diffuse the situation without antagonizing the FSA any further. In the end, they wound up calling attention to the explosives in the global media, and the FSA was forced to laud them as heroes who foiled a terrorist attack. Aeon smoothed ruffled feathers, with the typical mix of diplomacy and veiled threats, but the group was still less than welcome on American soil after that. </div> </div> </div>Interesting, you distracted the players with the FSA before they could get hung up on Proteus - a bit of cognitive swap-over in the new era. Do i understand your intention, or am i reading too far into it?<br /> I was expecting a lighter hearted piece, but you still seem to maintain much of the conspiratorial atmosphere. I wouldn't mind hearing you expand on why you did this piece (or any others) instead of Huang-Marr. Do you mind?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As for the Doyen, I'd actually like to see a Kosh out of them, even though we saw how that went for Kosh. I'm a fan of dissenting voices. Physically, they were described as balls of light and plasma, almost like miniature suns (part of why it was so easy for them to manipulate the Chromatics; they already worshipped Gods of Light, so physical embodiments of light claiming to speak for the gods had an easy time of it). How they looked before? Who knows? I hope to find out in the future, though. To my mind, they're the central villains of the era. The aberrants are nightmares and painful reminders of the past, but the games are very much about improving the future. The Doyen seemed dead-set on controlling that future, one way or the other. </div> </div> </div><br /> Introducing a Kosh-like ambassador for the Doyen might buy me enough time to properly figure out what they are/were/will be. It might require some interesting lead in to introducing the character - but it would certainly help to muddy the increasingly murky waters of Doyen motivations. But, the aliens of Aeon/Trinity were excellent creatures founded on some solid thought processes - any attempt i made to detail the Doyen would have needed to meet that high standard and it was an intimidating target to bear down upon.<br /> I was leaning toward a multi-dimensional approach, perhaps giving them a kind of external perspective on our universes space-time. Because the Hammersmith event messed up space-time in my version, they were unable to properly prevent the spread of taint at the source and due to causality must await the era after the N-Day effect subsided to act upon the local universe. The poor Doyen might have had a desperate few years after Venezuela before the dissonance in space-time locked them out for another century or so at least. Sleeper agents and life extension for said sleeper agents might be the final gift from the 'benefactors' going forward into an inspired 22nd century.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4458-nihilist","name":"Nihilist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4458_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-10T08:33:05-07:00","id":509866,"title":"","content":"Hey there! Sorry for the delay in response. One of my best friends got married yesterday, so I've been busy with groomsman duties. With their knot tied, I'm free!<br /> <br /> 1. Truthfully, I see the Colony as having some potential as a more compelling villain, and his sub-aberrants in particular. I just wanted a bit more out of the books for him. Perhaps next time.<br /> As for the creation of aberrants, I didn't use it as a sort of wish-fulfillment as much as the outside reflecting the inside of the aberrant in question. More than one would have preferred not to be mutated beyond recognition - one of the lead aberrants I ran on Chrome Prime even blamed humanity's exile of the aberrants for the mutations they suffer - but the quantum and the taint take the environment and the personality of the individual to create great and terrible things.<br /> 2. High quantum powers tend to confound hard science pretty hard. Not that there's anything wrong with hard science, mind. At best, universe creation is a sort of drastic, gonzo expansion of Schrodinger's Cat; if the cat is alive, then somewhere, in infinite quantum possibility, there must be a place where the cat is dead. Build a physical framework around that quantum possibility, and you have an entire universe made different by that cat. The metaphor's getting pretty stretched at this point, but you take my meaning.<br /> As a trilogy, I did feel there needs to be some sort of symmetry and consistency. Donighal was an integral part of the setting from the beginning, even though he technically spent much of the nova age in Chrysalis. Leaving him completely out of the Trinity era, after giving his ominous threat, just feels wasteful.<br /> 3. I did run some of them as self-aware, as far as their natural awesomeness goes, but not all. I feel their abilities are subtle enough that there's room in the setting for both. As for the dramatic editing, I never thought of that as a conscious thing; the story just adapts to the needs of its driving forces. It would be interesting to run it as a conscious mechanism, though.<br /> 4. There's something to be said for darkness, and the NPCs are not necessarily as capable of beating it back as the PCs are. There should be plenty of darkness and grey to provide contrast.<br /> 5. I assume Max and the rest of Aeon have a special place in the closest thing they could find to Hell for those in charge of those experiments. Honestly, those parts, to me, make the double Max explanation untenable to me. Even faced with the end of humanity, I simply can't picture him setting his idealism and humanity aside THAT far. Even knowing that hideous war is coming, I cannot see the two-fisted pulp hero and humanist paragon ordering torture and vile human experimentation. It just doesn't fit.<br /> 7. No attack read in that. Just trying to clarify my view.<br /> Exactly that. They already spent a long time fighting hidden conspiracies in their own backyards. They needed a bit of unequivocal evil to keep on-message. The FSA, especially since my group was entirely American (I am, funny enough, Venezuelan, but I've lived in the US all my life), fits the bill perfectly. A fascist military dictatorship where you have to literally pay for your rights? Perfect when you need to indulge in the urge to punch some Nazis.<br /> A Kosh-like ambassador would probably do a lot for making the Doyen factions feel less monolithic. That said, it might be important for it to end the same way for him as it did for Kosh, to make sure the players don't feel the Doyen as a species are well-meaning.<br /> Treating them as outsiders is an interesting idea. They're pretty much sub-quantum beings, so it's uncertain if they even understand quantum reality beyond abject terror. Your use of them could be a fun one, perhaps more so than the &quot;vanilla&quot; description of them as distant beings reacting to their first encounters with the aberrants by manipulating or attacking humanity directly. Either way, I see them as a bit of a psychomorph analogue to the aberrants: &quot;living&quot; examples of what happens when the powers of the Inspired create an attitude of superiority and distance from the wants and needs of &quot;ordinary&quot; living things. The Inspired PCs use their powers to help people and are heroes for it. The Doyen and the aberrants use their powers for their own selfish reasons, power for power's sake, and that really should make the PCs angry.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4732-drazosh","name":"Drazosh","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"50"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-12T14:06:38-07:00","id":512302,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Drazosh</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-\u00e6on/494207-my-aeon-marathon?p=509866#post509866\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hey there! Sorry for the delay in response. One of my best friends got married yesterday, so I've been busy with groomsman duties. With their knot tied, I'm free!</div> </div> </div> </div>All my best and/or condolences as required to your friend.<br /> -smile-<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 1. Truthfully, I see the Colony as having some potential as a more compelling villain, and his sub-aberrants in particular. I just wanted a bit more out of the books for him. Perhaps next time.<br /> As for the creation of aberrants, I didn't use it as a sort of wish-fulfillment as much as the outside reflecting the inside of the aberrant in question. More than one would have preferred not to be mutated beyond recognition - one of the lead aberrants I ran on Chrome Prime even blamed humanity's exile of the aberrants for the mutations they suffer - but the quantum and the taint take the environment and the personality of the individual to create great and terrible things. </div> </div> </div>All the tainted Aberrants i ran through the 2120s had a personalized slip into their own nasty syndromes. Highly tainted Aberrants i used as NPCs were replete with wierd and frightening mutations that served as warnings against too much Taint acquisition, but unless they kept their quantum low - the rules i used (from the Aberrant core book, i think) meant they would eventually succumb to their own power. It was entirely sympathetic, torturous and suited me just fine coming straight out of the WoD.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 2. High quantum powers tend to confound hard science pretty hard. Not that there's anything wrong with hard science, mind. At best, universe creation is a sort of drastic, gonzo expansion of Schrodinger's Cat; if the cat is alive, then somewhere, in infinite quantum possibility, there must be a place where the cat is dead. Build a physical framework around that quantum possibility, and you have an entire universe made different by that cat. The metaphor's getting pretty stretched at this point, but you take my meaning.<br /> As a trilogy, I did feel there needs to be some sort of symmetry and consistency. Donighal was an integral part of the setting from the beginning, even though he technically spent much of the nova age in Chrysalis. Leaving him completely out of the Trinity era, after giving his ominous threat, just feels wasteful. </div> </div> </div>I know, i took a good hard science sci-fi game and went right off a cliff by introducing Aberrants for a wow factor that was perhaps ill-conceived in retrospect. I could have a go with quantum jargon, sure. But at the time, it was the late 90s and i was even stupider than i am now (hard to believe, i know). Lucky the cat in the box can be stretched out as a metaphor, because it can at once be both illustrative and indecipherable as long as it stays in the box.<br /> -smile-<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3. I did run some of them as self-aware, as far as their natural awesomeness goes, but not all. I feel their abilities are subtle enough that there's room in the setting for both. As for the dramatic editing, I never thought of that as a conscious thing; the story just adapts to the needs of its driving forces. It would be interesting to run it as a conscious mechanism, though. </div> </div> </div>I would often half-seriously run old daredevils as having at least a peripheral awareness of their dramatic edits as improbable strings of unlikely luck in their favor. The 'Death Defiance' knack works kind of like a well-rehearsed Dramatic Edit, so i assumed that was a theme for Daredevils in particular - which would inevitably lead to some level of genre-awareness when reasoned out. The idea that extending this condition to levels as high or higher as Novas were to Stalwarts results in a temporal control possessed only by Mercer in canon always sounded fascinating, but i never really had a chance to explore that branch of the inspired as much as i would have liked to.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 4. There's something to be said for darkness, and the NPCs are not necessarily as capable of beating it back as the PCs are. There should be plenty of darkness and grey to provide contrast. </div> </div> </div>Then we agree. World of Brightness/Darkness is just too narrow a definition for the setting as a whole.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 5. I assume Max and the rest of Aeon have a special place in the closest thing they could find to Hell for those in charge of those experiments. Honestly, those parts, to me, make the double Max explanation untenable to me. Even faced with the end of humanity, I simply can't picture him setting his idealism and humanity aside THAT far. Even knowing that hideous war is coming, I cannot see the two-fisted pulp hero and humanist paragon ordering torture and vile human experimentation. It just doesn't fit. </div> </div> </div>Yeah, that stuck in my spokes too.<br /> Double slit Max actually allows Adventure! and Trinity to simply 'bifurcate' out of that aberrant era, it's the path of least resistance when sorting out the settings and if you don't intend to use Aberrant as written with the others, it's elegant too. I wanted something more baroque and personal involving a single Max in all three eras, so i tried to figure it out on my own, but never got all the way through.<br /> I had a lot of trouble fitting a time-travelling, near-baseline Mercer into the Aberrant material even without One Vorlons recollections - i was leaning pretty heavy towards Max not having access to the era due to N-Day eruption events being renewed every few years. At first this would have been deliberate by Donighal - but at some point he clearly lost the plot in the Teragen material - so later successive N-Day renewals that would be initiated by other novas are progressively corrupted versions of the original, like a game of chinese whispers. Which then contributes to a quantum pollutant feedback loop - forcing the relatively balanced creations of Divis Mal into the hopelessly tainted Aberrants of the war and exile eventually devolving into the sub-Aberrants we could see in the trinity material.<br /> Eventually Mercer would have returned and had the 'what have we done?' moment you mentioned in the Mercer thread, probably a few years prior to the war or possibly after it's already been going for a few years and started to clean house. No more utopia, proteus, or any of it - Aeon society reunion, back-to-basics. Somehow, Proteus itself or an organization sharing the name earns the (possibly grudging) trust of what eventually becomes the 2120 Aeon council - hopefully with a dramatically sufficient demonstration of the value in tolerating it after the acts perpertrated under that name within living memory.<br /> But i could never come up with an act of redemption big enough for their genocide, a lack of imagination on my part perhaps.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 7. No attack read in that. Just trying to clarify my view.<br /> Exactly that. They already spent a long time fighting hidden conspiracies in their own backyards. They needed a bit of unequivocal evil to keep on-message. The FSA, especially since my group was entirely American (I am, funny enough, Venezuelan, but I've lived in the US all my life), fits the bill perfectly. A fascist military dictatorship where you have to literally pay for your rights? Perfect when you need to indulge in the urge to punch some Nazis.<br /> A Kosh-like ambassador would probably do a lot for making the Doyen factions feel less monolithic. That said, it might be important for it to end the same way for him as it did for Kosh, to make sure the players don't feel the Doyen as a species are well-meaning. </div> </div> </div>Must be a little ray of sunlight to read or hear the Venezuela effect come up, since its such a potentially fun and hopeful event in the setting.<br /> -smile-<br /> There are heaps of great villains in the 2120s, no need to serve up Aeon. I had to split hairs to extract Aeon from wrongdoing in Huang-Marr (they <i>were</i> the ones investigating it luckily), to prevent a breakdown in trust between the players and their sponsors. Fun roleplay with Hector Ramirez, but it was kink enough for possible derailment, if not for group experience and careful reading of the material.<br /> All this talk about a Doyen 'Kosh' is making me want to write up a sandbox-style floating encounter for the reveal. Maybe even some lead-up sub-plot flowcharts...<br /> -blush-<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Treating them as outsiders is an interesting idea. They're pretty much sub-quantum beings, so it's uncertain if they even understand quantum reality beyond abject terror. Your use of them could be a fun one, perhaps more so than the &quot;vanilla&quot; description of them as distant beings reacting to their first encounters with the aberrants by manipulating or attacking humanity directly. Either way, I see them as a bit of a psychomorph analogue to the aberrants: &quot;living&quot; examples of what happens when the powers of the Inspired create an attitude of superiority and distance from the wants and needs of &quot;ordinary&quot; living things. The Inspired PCs use their powers to help people and are heroes for it. The Doyen and the aberrants use their powers for their own selfish reasons, power for power's sake, and that really should make the PCs angry. </div> </div> </div>Thanks. The more we talk about this the more i want to digress to a pure Doyen thread, lots of head canon rattling around up here on my part.<br /> I wasn't quite sure how their multi-dimensionality would work, they clearly manifested most clearly in the detectable dimensions through the sub-quantum medium - but if a theoretical (insert jargon name) dimension were to exist outside of three/four dimensional 'quantum' space-time without interacting then a kind of omniversal perspective could've been a much more dangerous and long term problem for the 2120s and perhaps <i>all</i> of time to come. Trying to box with an opponent whose reach extends from beyond the physical universe seems like a good addition to the Trinity 'gallery of rogues' - a foe only psions can hope to defeat in memorable psionic flurries in the centre of their invisible webs of manipulations.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4458-nihilist","name":"Nihilist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4458_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"259"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}